WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.282
This slide shows why we should estimate the size of a cross join before running it.

00:00:04.282 --> 00:00:08.292
Two rows crossed with three rows produce six combinations.

00:00:08.292 --> 00:00:12.807
One hundred crossed with one hundred produces ten thousand combinations.

00:00:12.807 --> 00:00:17.662
Ten thousand crossed with ten thousand produces one hundred million combinations.

00:00:17.662 --> 00:00:23.282
The formula is simply N times M, where N is the number of rows on the left and

00:00:23.282 --> 00:00:26.012
M is the number of rows on the right.

00:00:26.012 --> 00:00:31.492
The calculation is easy, but the growth can become very large very quickly.

00:00:31.492 --> 00:00:33.957
CROSS JOIN is not inherently bad.

00:00:33.957 --> 00:00:37.037
It is useful when every combination is intentional.

00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:42.862
For example, you might generate every store and every month, every product and every region,

00:00:42.862 --> 00:00:45.472
or every department and every appointment slot.

00:00:45.472 --> 00:00:49.962
The danger appears when a normal join condition is accidentally omitted.

00:00:49.962 --> 00:00:54.227
If you expected a few thousand rows and suddenly get millions,

00:00:54.227 --> 00:00:58.507
one of the first things to check is whether a relationship condition is missing and the query has

00:00:58.507 --> 00:01:01.910
effectively created a Cartesian product.
